978abc-6a14-5845-a38a-8c057e2a5b8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-253372" :question/problem "Given that\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}],$\nfind $\\operatorname{cos}⁡D.$\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡D=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a diagonal matrix $D$ with diagonal entries ${\\lambda }_{1},…,{\\lambda }_{n},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(D)=[\\begin{aligned}f({\\lambda }_{1}) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & f({\\lambda }_{n})\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡D & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ce12d6f-8d1c-52f3-992f-534b9d91dda9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Block"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "277d92aa-737c-57ec-bec7-b26a7d7dbcea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "277d92aa-737c-57ec-bec7-b26a7d7dbcea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377805} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cd0e72c-2690-5e9a-bec1-c61ca8e5a7e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377804} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ddb6356-1a76-5e02-ae2a-3998b9dde5d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ddb6356-1a76-5e02-ae2a-3998b9dde5d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377807} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ad8b78a-9762-5531-a9e0-74658bcdc023" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377806} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "561e4323-6743-57f4-98ff-31bb4930bd1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "561e4323-6743-57f4-98ff-31bb4930bd1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377809} :answer-field/id #uuid "24748f1d-ab40-5022-a242-cb5a871aef6e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377808} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc9ddd7a-caac-5803-a7ac-b37bc6f943e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc9ddd7a-caac-5803-a7ac-b37bc6f943e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377811} :answer-field/id #uuid "41efa50a-860f-5487-8761-c4524c9b3c96" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c95d823-6d07-5f58-aea5-f1021bae6636" :question/math-academy-id "q-253400" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | $-3$ | $0$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3{J}_{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-3$ |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-4}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x,$ $n=4,$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nSince $n=4,$ we need to compute the first $4-1=3$ derivatives of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =27\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & \\frac{1}{2!}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & \\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & \\frac{1}{2!}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & f(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & -\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & \\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\text{ } \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & -\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 0 & \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ce12d6f-8d1c-52f3-992f-534b9d91dda9" :knowledge-point/title