/id #edb/ref 17592186377821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8864def9-1e32-5e54-8d4d-63599c29e9d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377821} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2de5c2e-45c7-5c72-a4c7-5271eb93b28b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377820} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a68c184-d7d1-58a4-8f40-8e3ae182db29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a68c184-d7d1-58a4-8f40-8e3ae182db29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377823} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef67ea9e-ab09-5e57-a0be-bdf8acff3892" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377822} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daf631fb-492a-5bfc-a6a6-19d3f6ae5078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daf631fb-492a-5bfc-a6a6-19d3f6ae5078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377825} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5b517d9-723d-53c9-919b-836fd0969989" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd8827a3-a130-54f6-94e7-122f2ae40017" :question/math-academy-id "q-253421" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\sqrt{{J}_{3}(4)}=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)=\\sqrt{x},$ $n=3,$ and $\\lambda =4.$\n\nSince $n=3,$ we need to compute the first $3-1=2$ derivatives of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{4x\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(2)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(4) & {f}^{′}(4) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(4)}{2!} \\\\ 0 & f(4) & {f}^{′}(4) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4} & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}} & -\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 4\\cdot 4\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{4} & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ 0 & 2 & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "706303c1-0e4c-55f7-8b62-5094938f85bc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b11a1cf5-3316-564d-a498-7ded96a2f497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\pi }{4} & -5\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & -\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377828} {:answer/id #uuid "76917f05-cea9-559b-bc46-9eda73854e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{4} & -5\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi }{2} & \\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377829} {:answer/id #uuid "fd706756-d86b-5a27-a7bb-dbf5841acd53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\pi }{4} & -3\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi }{2} & \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377830} {:answer/id #uuid "e7aaa5d4-5fd1-5e3c-bd2b-a47ab8d1b971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\pi }{4} & -3\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & -\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377831} {:answer/id #uuid "adc076e8-e4d5-5e8e-b2ad-6a83d9b5f192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\pi }{4} & -5\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & -\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7aaa5d4-5fd1-5e3c-bd2b-a47ab8d1b971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\pi }{4} & -3\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & -\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377831} :answer-field/id #uuid "abbbf68b-fe15-5d64-bc2a-24594aeba9f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80514654-23ca-5627-94ac-a61bb92cd6ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-253441" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -12 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $K=PD{P}^{-1},$ find $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡K.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡K$ and the given matrix $K,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡K=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(PD{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (\\operatorname{arctan}⁡D)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡D=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{3}{1\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡K & =P\\cdot (\\operatorname{arctan}⁡D)\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{4} & -\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4} & -\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\pi }{4} & -3\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & -\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "706303c1-0e4c-55f7-8b62-5094938f85bc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8b14a67-ed68-5042-9ea0-a2864f91278b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377835} {:answer/id #uuid "9baff02a-ec10-50b0-8142-6770602c78c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377836} {:answer/id #uuid "28ba76ec-316e-5b29-ad07-60db7d552254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377837} {:answer/id #uuid "dae02d50-bda7-5f98-87e4-558e4c16c161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377838} {:answer/id #uuid "f08e0ab5-8dca-5d58-bd8e-b4e98c2d1b17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dae02d50-bda7-5f98-87e4-558e4c16c161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377838} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cc4661f-379a-58a4-bb2e-24fbebbba85e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b4f91ee-fa00-5348-8d83-7a70812f2366" :question/math-academy-id "q-253480" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & 2\\pi  \\\\ 0 & 2\\pi \\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\pi \\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $C=PD{P}^{-1},$ find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡C.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡C$ and the given matrix $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡C=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(PD{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡D)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡D=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi \\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡C & =P\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡D)\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "706303c1-0e4c-55f7-8b62-5094938f85bc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd567233-3dfc-5c47-8bf4-b9d93d1981d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377842} {:answer/id #uuid "c87a786b-4462-508e-b25d-66b88048114f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377843} {:answer/id #uuid "d18d5f0e-63b4-5c32-82ab-fa0a33ac46cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377844} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc436b1-04e5-548c-b86c-485388784e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377845} {:answer/id #uuid "4f508220-71ff-5a6e-9a3a-ed2983a2c405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f508220-71ff-5a6e-9a3a-ed2983a2c405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377846} :answer-field/id #uuid "780799bb-ced2-57d3-8a68-e9c1f6929f7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee07f367-bf7f-5b22-855b-3a1b4562e7ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-253508" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $M=PJ{P}^{-1},$ find $\\operatorname{ln}⁡M.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and the given matrix $M,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡M=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(PJ{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡J)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $1$s on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡J=[\\begin{aligned}f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡J=[\\begin{aligned}f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) & \\frac{1}{(1)} \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡M & =P\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡J)\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "706303c1-0e4c-55f7-8b62-5094938f85bc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42b2cb1d-a6f8-59fd-ad04-fe72a3a6e2bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi -6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\pi +6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377849} {:answer/id #uuid "0682f98f-a714-5d2a-b7c4-0843b65b3c42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi +6 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\pi -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377850} {:answer/id #uuid "bb8819e6-47fa-527f-b4f4-62d3affd2af4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\pi +6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\pi -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377851} {:answer/id #uuid "df5c4e6a-e8f5-5cf6-9eca-71fd4005f749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\pi +3 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 2\\pi -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377852} {:answer/id #uuid "1ddde711-a147-5660-80e9-e005d686dc63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi -3 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\pi +3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0682f98f-a714-5d2a-b7c4-0843b65b3c42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi +6 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\pi -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377850} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3624ebb-47a8-5219-9db9-56774dfa0e07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "462adc62-6b27-5233-9c9d-d820967cef9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-253677" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nG=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}+2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 2 & \\,\\,\\sqrt{3}-2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\,1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $G=PJ{P}^{-1},$ find $12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡G.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ and the given matrix $G,$ we have\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡G=12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(PJ{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡J)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $\\sqrt{3}$ on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\nf(J)=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{3}) & {f}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\sqrt{3})\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+1}$ So, we get\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡J=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{3}) & {f}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\sqrt{3})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi  & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\pi \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡G & =P\\cdot (12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡J)\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4\\pi  & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\pi \\end{aligned}]\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi +3 & 4\\pi -3 \\\\ 4\\pi  & -4\\pi \\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi +6 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\pi -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "706303c1-0e4c-55f7-8b62-5094938f85bc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e1c7cf3-92e2-51e3-a512-f4a45fc36b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9 \\\\ -9 & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377856} {:answer/id #uuid "3fbc4e8d-7575-5bc7-ae07-4e2015667330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377857} {:answer/id #uuid "1e71b3c4-247e-5879-bddb-4d38d6a57cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377858} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5e732e-35c6-5712-b7dc-3f9801d323d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+9 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377859} {:answer/id #uuid "521002f2-0a33-59fe-9439-af5ace860728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "521002f2-0a33-59fe-9439-af5ace860728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377860} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c546ff5-7912-5a03-8315-41772f5e05eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01aa6efd-1aa0-5169-9c59-06c6dc6237ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-253682" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $K=PJ{P}^{-1},$ find ${3}^{K/2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)={3}^{x/2}$ and the given matrix $K,$ we have\n\n$$\n{3}^{K/2}={3}^{\\frac{1}{2}PJ{P}^{-1}}=P\\cdot ({3}^{J/2})\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $4$s on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\nf(J)=[\\begin{aligned}f(4) & {f}^{′}(4) \\\\ 0 & f(4)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{2}.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{3}^{J/2}=[\\begin{aligned}f(4) & {f}^{′}(4) \\\\ 0 & f(4)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & \\frac{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{2} \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{K/2} & =P\\cdot ({3}^{J/2})\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & \\frac{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{2} \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}18-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-9 \\\\ -18 & 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87883002-67eb-5111-b15a-c5941bb33f9b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3b9c9bc-fc2d-5a43-afaf-c48f82ec5dcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377863} {:answer/id #uuid "880b964d-b054-534d-980a-1433b3bb1d6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3b9c9bc-fc2d-5a43-afaf-c48f82ec5dcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377863} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ab94b6a-eeb9-5739-bb02-387e2214183a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377862} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d45fa92a-1daf-58b0-8780-37cd605f24b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d45fa92a-1daf-58b0-8780-37cd605f24b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377866} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eae5557-a5c5-5adc-b4e1-491957073b23" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377865} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d68f49e-c8c7-5db2-a2e2-aa274d0ba361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d68f49e-c8c7-5db2-a2e2-aa274d0ba361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377868} :answer-field/id #uuid "193b39e5-8f9c-514f-b17d-b992f47bbf6e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377867} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acb0b110-c093-5a90-9966-a022078e05ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acb0b110-c093-5a90-9966-a022078e05ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377870} :answer-field/id #uuid "896b99b6-5207-56d1-8b56-e545ed8107a1" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "050598da-85d2-5e2a-a713-ba0c50acc8ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-253712" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denotes the $n\\times n$ Jordan block matrix with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | $8$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(3))}^{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $8$ | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $27$ | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $27$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(3))^{3} & ={([\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(2) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{2}(3)\\end{aligned}])}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}({J}_{2}(2))^{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & ({J}_{2}(3))^{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =({J}_{2}(2))^{3}\\,⊕\\,({J}_{2}(3))^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{2}(2),$ we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda =2.$ Since $n=2,$ we need to compute the first derivative of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}(2)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(2) & {f}^{′}(2) \\\\ 0 & f(2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{3} & 3\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 0 & {2}^{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{2}(3),$ we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda =3.$ Since $n=2,$ we need the first derivative of $f$ only, which we have already computed. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}(3)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(3) & {f}^{′}(3) \\\\ 0 & f(3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3} & 3\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ 0 & {3}^{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}27 & 27 \\\\ 0 & 27\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(3))^{3}=({J}_{2}(2))^{3}\\,⊕\\,({J}_{2}(3))^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 12 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 27 & 27 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 27\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87883002-67eb-5111-b15a-c5941bb33f9b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90622f57-fb62-5f01-924c-457c522556ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377873} {:answer/id #uuid "c41c5d5f-905e-5f68-8366-3739d3966fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90622f57-fb62-5f01-924c-457c522556ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377873} :answer-field/id #uuid "99539b59-34fa-59e1-a999-08ca44307d55" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377872} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c755f95-a386-5515-a171-bbea1c7e7d57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c755f95-a386-5515-a171-bbea1c7e7d57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377876} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a73015a-d8e5-5e90-9d37-7807181c27bd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377875} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6429ad2-8589-5dd3-86de-a5896194ce4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6429ad2-8589-5dd3-86de-a5896194ce4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377878} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d2ac9e3-5c28-5e7f-968a-7aac7f03c341" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377877} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f885a00-978a-54a0-80ff-95bceee0041c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f885a00-978a-54a0-80ff-95bceee0041c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377880} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd2c7ab7-3ce6-5511-9de1-fc05518cfa21" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2ba9164-73a5-5ac3-b32f-c75e02587453" :question/math-academy-id "q-253873" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | $3$ | {{field-1}} | $\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${3}^{({J}_{3}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2))}=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $3$ | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $3$ | $0$ |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-4}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2({J}_{3}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2))} & ={3}^{([\\begin{aligned}{J}_{3}(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{1}(2)\\end{aligned}])} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{({J}_{3}(1))} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {3}^{({J}_{1}(2))}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={3}^{({J}_{3}(1))}\\,⊕\\,{3}^{({J}_{1}(2))}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{3}(1),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ Since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivative of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={3}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={3}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(1)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) & \\frac{1}{2!}{f}^{″}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{1} & {3}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {3}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2} \\\\ 0 & {3}^{1} & {3}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {3}^{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & \\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(2),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =1.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(2)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{(2)}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{({J}_{3}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2))} & ={3}^{({J}_{3}(1))}\\,⊕\\,{3}^{({J}_{1}(2))} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & \\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87883002-67eb-5111-b15a-c5941bb33f9b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "924d4dc6-9636-5495-8beb-c36fcf1926ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "924d4dc6-9636-5495-8beb-c36fcf1926ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377883} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0e5a2cc-0dc2-5ed6-b30e-6469780d2de9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377882} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54b6728e-4ca7-5a85-8054-380091ef3456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54b6728e-4ca7-5a85-8054-380091ef3456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377885} :answer-field/id #uuid "d46816a9-b2b5